{#
  The governed data-display component layer (`U1-02`, active `RRA-012`).

  One macro per concept FR-092 names, so a figure reads the same way wherever it
  appears and a slice adding a customer-visible figure has one place to render it.

  **These are macros rather than a Python module for two reasons, and both are
  load-bearing.** `RRA-012`'s Scope authorizes the rendering templates, `report.css`,
  `wording.py`'s chrome labels and one binding point in `html.py` -- and no new Python
  module; a `components.py` would be outside the specification. And a macro's output is
  markup because the macro *is* source, while every value inside it still passes through
  the same unconditional escaping as a bare table cell. A Python string of markup would
  either reach the reader as `&lt;td` or require `|safe`, and `html.py:23` records why
  the second is refused: "a page with one `|safe` in it has an escaping convention, not
  an escaping guarantee." This file adds no `|safe`, exactly as `_chart.svg.j2` does not.

  **No macro here computes, rounds, or reformats anything** (FR-093). Each renders the
  value it is handed. `FigureCell` carries no `value` field precisely so a renderer
  cannot format a number itself; these macros keep that guarantee rather than restoring
  the ability to break it.

  **Every word a macro renders comes from `chrome`** -- the mapping `html.py` binds into
  the context. Metric, refusal and caveat wording is `RRA-009`'s and `RRA-011`'s and is
  never authored here; what this layer adds is its own chrome, the text naming a region
  or state of the interface (FR-095a), registered under `chrome.component` and
  `chrome.component_state`.

  **Which surface a component appears on is decided by `RRA-009`'s visibility matrix,
  not by this file.** A citation identifier and every version and count in the bundle
  identity are tier A, Audit, so the evidence link, version label and coverage
  indicator render in the evidence region -- the Technical Evidence page and the printed
  appendix -- and never on the business page. The figure, badge, quality summary and
  refusal panel render on the business page. One layer, two regions.
#}

{#
  A governed figure, as one document prints it.

  `text` arrives already formatted by the analysis that published it, and is emitted
  unchanged. `data-component` is the marker every surface-inventory guard reads: a
  figure rendered without it is a figure bypassing this layer, which FR-101 refuses.

  **Two existing tests had to move for this, and the reason is worth recording.**
  `test_rra006_html_surface` and `test_rra006_series_pivot` counted figures with the
  literal opening tag, class and closing bracket included, so *no* added attribute
  could preserve that substring in any order. Both now count by the class attribute,
  which is the property they were always asserting.
#}
{% macro figure(text) -%}
<td data-component="figure" class="figure">{{ text if text is not none else "" }}</td>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  A section's state, named in words rather than signalled by colour alone (FR-100).

  `state` is a governed section-state constant the caller already holds -- the same
  one `report.html.j2` compares against `refused_state` to decide whether a section
  renders figures or a refusal. The audit context carries no state field at all
  (`html.py:519`, tier Internal), and this macro asks for none: it takes the constant
  the caller compared and looks up its word.

  An unrecognised state refuses rather than rendering the code string, an empty element,
  or a blank (FR-094). `chrome.component_state[...]` is a plain mapping lookup, so an
  unknown key becomes the environment's `StrictUndefined` and raises `UndefinedError`
  at render -- the refusal is the environment's own, not a message this layer invents.

  No `tabindex`: a badge is read, not operated, and putting it in the tab order would
  add a focus stop with no action behind it.
#}
{% macro status_badge(state, chrome) -%}
<span data-component="status-badge" class="badge badge--{{ state }}">{{
  chrome.component_state[state] }}</span>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  What one report answered, answered with a caveat, and refused.

  A grouping of outcomes the page already renders -- three counts of sections, handed
  in by the caller, which selects them with template filters over the section views it
  already iterates. Nothing here is a figure: these are counts of headings on this page,
  and the macro computes none of them. It expresses availability and never certainty,
  so there is no score, percentage, or confidence anywhere in it.
#}
{% macro quality_summary(answered, caveated, refused, chrome) -%}
<dl data-component="quality-summary" class="quality-summary"
    aria-label="{{ chrome.component.quality_summary }}">
<div><dt>{{ chrome.component.quality_answered }}</dt><dd>{{ answered }}</dd></div>
<div><dt>{{ chrome.component.quality_caveated }}</dt><dd>{{ caveated }}</dd></div>
<div><dt>{{ chrome.component.quality_refused }}</dt><dd>{{ refused }}</dd></div>
</dl>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  A refused section's governed reason.

  `prose` is `RRA-009`'s, reaching this macro through `chrome.refusal_prose` exactly as
  the hand-built paragraph it replaces did. This layer authors no refusal wording; it
  gives the existing wording one consistent place to appear, and a text label beside
  it so the state is never carried by the rule colour alone (FR-100).
#}
{% macro refusal_panel(prose, chrome) -%}
<p data-component="refusal-panel" class="refused" role="note">
<span class="refused__label">{{ chrome.component.refusal_label }}</span>
{{ prose }}
</p>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  The link from a figure to the citation that supports it.

  `citation_id` is the identifier the package already published, and the link's text
  is that identifier: an auditor joins on it, so hiding it behind a label would remove
  the one value the column exists to show. The target is the citation's own entry in
  the same document -- `_evidence.html.j2` gives every citation `id="citation-…"` --
  which is why this component lives in the evidence region only: the business page
  carries no citation anchors, and a link to an anchor a document lacks is a dead link.
#}
{% macro evidence_link(citation_id) -%}
<a data-component="evidence-link" class="evidence-link"
   href="#citation-{{ citation_id }}"><code>{{ citation_id }}</code></a>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  A governed version, as the bundle identity records it.

  Rendered as given. A version is an identity, not a number to format.
#}
{% macro version_label(version, chrome) -%}
<span data-component="version-label" class="version-label">
<span class="version-label__name">{{ chrome.component.version_label }}</span>
<code>{{ version }}</code>
</span>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  What the package was computed over, at the package level.

  **Deliberately not per figure** (FR-097). A `Fact` records no population or basis
  identifier, and `RRA-011` declines to invent that relationship rather than infer it.
  This states what the report was computed over; it never claims to state what one
  number was computed over. The value is the identity's own `row_count`, rendered as
  given.
#}
{% macro coverage_indicator(scope, chrome) -%}
<span data-component="coverage-indicator" class="coverage">
<span class="coverage__label">{{ chrome.component.coverage }}</span>
<code>{{ scope }}</code>
</span>
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  The evidence drawer (`U1-04`): what the governed records say about one figure, beside
  that figure (FR-096).

  `given` is one entry of the audit context's `evidence` -- `RRA-013`'s per-citation
  record with its definition resolved -- taken as-is; `coverage` is the context's
  bundle-level `coverage`, handed in **separately** because `RRA-013` FR-104 forbids
  copying it into an entry. The macro reads exactly the keys it renders -- definition,
  formula version, inputs, citation from `given`; manifest identity and signatures from
  `coverage` -- and nothing else, so a field a future projection adds cannot reach the
  page through it.
  It has no population or basis input at all (FR-097): a `Fact` carries no population
  identifier, and `RRA-011` declines to invent that relationship.

  **An absent field is stated, never blank and never refused (FR-096a).** The catalog
  returns `inputs: None` for a derived analysis figure because no retained record states
  them, and `_cited_figure`'s docstring records why re-deriving them is forbidden. Such a
  field renders `chrome.component.unavailable` with `data-state="unavailable"`. That word
  is not the refusal label and nothing here carries the `refused` class: the catalog
  declining to state something is not a refusal. An *empty* inputs list is a different
  finding again -- stated, and empty -- and renders `chrome.none`.

  **Keyboard behaviour comes from the element, not from a script (FR-098).** `html.py`
  bundles no JavaScript and a test refuses `<script>`. A native `<details>` is reachable
  and dismissible from the keyboard through its `<summary>`, and the opener keeps focus
  when toggled, so "return focus to the opener" holds by construction. The summary is the
  drawer's only control; nothing in the body carries `tabindex`. Escape-to-close from
  inside the body would need a script no active specification admits on this surface,
  and is recorded in the plan as a known limit.

  The version and the citation render through their own `U1-02` components (FR-092): the
  version label carries its own word, so it stands above the field list rather than
  inside a `<dt>`/`<dd>` pair that would say "Version" twice.

  **`open` is for paper.** A closed `<details>` prints collapsed, so a drawer in the printed
  appendix would carry its opener and none of its evidence. The print surface passes
  `open=true`; the web surfaces leave the default, so a reader opens what they want. This
  is the one presentation difference between the two, and it is stated as a parameter
  rather than inferred from a stylesheet no CSS rule could implement.

  **Placed beside every figure row of the evidence region** by `_evidence.html.j2`, once
  `RRA-013` supplied the data. Until then it was placed nowhere, and a guard held that
  line: an unsupplied drawer would have rendered every field unavailable for a reason
  FR-096a does not mean.
#}
{% macro evidence_drawer(given, chrome, coverage, open=false) -%}
<details data-component="evidence-drawer" class="drawer"{% if open %} open{% endif %}>
<summary class="drawer__opener">{{ chrome.component.drawer_open }}</summary>
<div class="drawer__version">{{ version_label(given.formula_version, chrome) }}</div>
<dl class="drawer__fields">
<div>
<dt>{{ chrome.component.definition }}</dt>
{% if given.definition is none -%}
<dd data-state="unavailable">{{ chrome.component.unavailable }}</dd>
{%- else -%}
<dd>{{ given.definition }}</dd>
{%- endif %}
</div>
<div>
<dt>{{ chrome.component.inputs }}</dt>
{% if given.inputs is none -%}
<dd data-state="unavailable">{{ chrome.component.unavailable }}</dd>
{%- elif not given.inputs -%}
<dd>{{ chrome.none }}</dd>
{%- else -%}
<dd>{% for input in given.inputs %}<code>{{ input }}</code>{{ " " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</dd>
{%- endif %}
</div>
<div>
<dt>{{ chrome.component.coverage }}</dt>
{% if coverage.manifest_identity is none -%}
<dd data-state="unavailable">{{ chrome.component.unavailable }}</dd>
{%- else -%}
<dd><code>{{ coverage.manifest_identity }}</code></dd>
{%- endif %}
</div>
{#
  Signatures are a second field, not a suffix of the first. A manifest can be present and
  cover none of the package's admitted event kinds or statuses -- `_signatures_of` then
  returns nothing while the identity stays populated -- and folding an empty list into the
  identity's cell made unproven coverage look present. Stated in three ways: no manifest is
  unavailable; a manifest with no compatible signature is `chrome.none`; otherwise the list.
#}
<div>
<dt>{{ chrome.component.coverage_signatures }}</dt>
{% if coverage.manifest_identity is none -%}
<dd data-state="unavailable">{{ chrome.component.unavailable }}</dd>
{%- elif not coverage.signatures -%}
<dd data-state="none">{{ chrome.none }}</dd>
{%- else -%}
<dd>{% for signature in coverage.signatures %}<code>{{ signature }}</code>{{ " " if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</dd>
{%- endif %}
</div>
<div>
<dt>{{ chrome.citation }}</dt>
<dd>{{ evidence_link(given.citation_id) }}</dd>
</div>
</dl>
</details>
{%- endmacro %}
